.TH NP_SINK 3 "June 2026" "netpipe v0.1.0" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
np_sink_pcap, np_sink_pcapng, np_sink_json, np_sink_hex, np_sink_stats,
np_sink_null, np_sink_pretty, np_sink_tuntap, np_sink_socket,
np_sink_free, np_pipeline_add_sink \- netpipe packet output sinks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <netpipe.h>
.PP
.BI "np_sink_t *np_sink_pcap(const char *"   path ");"
.br
.BI "np_sink_t *np_sink_pcapng(const char *" path ");"
.br
.BI "np_sink_t *np_sink_json(const char *"   path ");"
.br
.BI "np_sink_t *np_sink_hex(const char *"    path ");"
.br
.BI "np_sink_t *np_sink_stats(const char *"  path ");"
.br
.BI "np_sink_t *np_sink_null(void);"
.br
.BI "np_sink_t *np_sink_pretty(const char *" path ");"
.br
.BI "NP_EXPERIMENTAL np_sink_t *np_sink_tuntap(const char *"  uri ");"
.br
.BI "NP_EXPERIMENTAL np_sink_t *np_sink_socket(const char *"  uri ");"
.br
.BI "void       np_sink_free(np_sink_t *"    s ");"
.br
.BI "np_err_t   np_pipeline_add_sink(np_pipeline_t *" pl ", np_sink_t *" s ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions create and attach output sinks to a pipeline.
Sinks write, format, or transmit processed packets.
A single pipeline can have multiple sinks simultaneously; each packet is
delivered to all sinks in the order they were added.
.PP
For the
.IR path
argument, use
.B \-
to write to standard output; pass
.B /dev/null
or
.B NULL
to discard.
.SS Stable sinks
.PP
The following sinks are part of the stable API and will not be removed or
have their signatures altered without a
.B NETPIPE_VERSION_MAJOR
bump.
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_pcap ()
function creates a binary PCAP sink that writes packets in the standard
libpcap format to the file at
.IR path .
The output is compatible with Wireshark and tcpdump.
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_pcapng ()
function creates a PCAP-NG sink, recording interface-description and
name-resolution blocks in addition to packet data.
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_json ()
function creates a JSON formatter sink.
It writes one JSON object per line (NDJSON) containing packet summaries,
decoded layers, and protocol data to
.IR path .
See
.BR netpipe-python (7)
for the full JSON schema.
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_hex ()
function creates an annotated hex-dump sink that writes human-readable
representations with protocol layer markers to
.IR path .
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_stats ()
function creates a statistics logger that periodically (every 5 seconds)
dumps packet counters and protocol distributions to
.IR path .
A final summary is written when the pipeline stops.
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_null ()
function creates a null sink that discards all packet data.
It is useful for benchmarking processors, or when using the
.B \-stats
option to collect statistics without storing packets.
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_pretty ()
function creates a tshark-style single-line summary sink that prints
one formatted text line per packet to
.IR path .
.SS Experimental sinks
.PP
The following sinks are marked
.B NP_EXPERIMENTAL
because their URI formats and exact behaviour may change in future releases
without a major-version bump.
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_tuntap ()
function creates a virtual-network-interface injector sink.
The parameter
.I uri
selects the target device:
.BR "tun://tun0"
for a TUN (Layer-3) device or
.BR "tap://tap0"
for a TAP (Layer-2) device.
Packets written to this sink are injected directly into the Linux
kernel routing subsystem.
Requires
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
or root.
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_socket ()
function creates a network-forwarding sink.
The parameter
.I uri
specifies the destination:
.BR "socket://192.168.1.100:9999" .
The sink opens a TCP connection and streams raw packets preceded by
standard PCAP global and record headers so that Wireshark or netpipe can
consume the remote stream directly.
.SS Ownership
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_free ()
function releases resources for a sink that was
.I not
added to a pipeline.
Sinks successfully passed to
.BR np_pipeline_add_sink ()
are owned by the pipeline and will be freed automatically when
.BR np_pipeline_free ()
is called; the caller must not free them.
.PP
The
.BR np_pipeline_add_sink ()
function attaches an output sink to the pipeline.
.SH RETURN VALUES
Sink creation functions return a pointer to a newly allocated
.B np_sink_t
on success, or
.B NULL
on failure.
.PP
.BR np_pipeline_add_sink ()
returns
.B NP_OK
on success, or a negative
.B np_err_t
code on failure (see
.BR libnetpipe (3)).
.SH NOTES
.B np_sink_tuntap ()
and
.B np_sink_socket ()
are tagged
.BR NP_EXPERIMENTAL .
Do not rely on their URI syntax or output format remaining stable across
minor releases.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libnetpipe (3),
.BR np_pipeline (3),
.BR netpipe-python (7)
